    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im studying for my security+ and im a bit stuck. if i am already hashing a password, why do i actually need to add a salt to it? wouldnt the hash alone be enough to keep it secure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The hash alone isn't enough because identical passwords result in identical hashes, which is vulnerable to pre-computed attacks like rainbow tables. Adding a salt introduces a unique, randomly generated string to each password before hashing. This ensures that even if two users have the same password, their stored hashes will be completely different, significantly increasing security against rainbow table attacks.",
